WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.851
Now the other direction: the data is in your database, and a partner expects a FundsXML file.

2
00:00:05.901 --> 00:00:14.678
In this film you see the ways to generate FundsXML, the mapping behind it, how to serve several partners from one generator, and how to test the result.

3
00:00:14.728 --> 00:00:21.472
The generator of the complete example writes from the database of the last two films, and you can download all of it.

4
00:00:22.722 --> 00:00:25.034
Start with the target, not with the code.

5
00:00:25.084 --> 00:00:33.904
Take an official example file that comes close to what your partner expects, for example one of the realistic samples in the FundsXML examples repository.

6
00:00:33.954 --> 00:00:37.648
It shows which elements a complete delivery uses, in which order.

7
00:00:37.698 --> 00:00:42.545
Agree it with your partner, and you know what to build before the first line of code.

8
00:00:42.595 --> 00:00:47.556
The complete example keeps the files its generator must produce right next to the code.

9
00:00:48.906 --> 00:00:53.037
Then the mapping: for each element, where does the value come from?

10
00:00:53.087 --> 00:01:01.023
A simple table does the job: the FundsXML path, the source in the database, and the rule, such as a conversion or a fixed value.

11
00:01:01.073 --> 00:01:04.580
This table is the most important document of the project.

12
00:01:04.630 --> 00:01:09.010
It is also what the business side reviews, long before any file exists.

13
00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:12.754
The example keeps it as a file of its own, next to the code.

14
00:01:14.104 --> 00:01:15.535
Which data goes to whom?

15
00:01:15.585 --> 00:01:22.075
The partner register answers it: which funds a partner receives, which kinds of data, and which templates.

16
00:01:22.125 --> 00:01:31.021
In the example, the distributor receives the umbrella and the multi-asset fund, with three days of prices, the documents and the E M T and E E T.

17
00:01:31.071 --> 00:01:35.154
The insurer receives two funds with their portfolios and the T P T.

18
00:01:35.204 --> 00:01:39.658
Adding a fund for a partner is a change in the register, not in the code.

19
00:01:41.008 --> 00:01:43.903
There are three common ways to write the file.

20
00:01:43.953 --> 00:01:49.089
Templates write the XML directly from the query results; simple and easy to read.

21
00:01:49.139 --> 00:01:54.531
X S L T transforms an intermediate XML, for example from the database, into FundsXML.

22
00:01:54.581 --> 00:02:03.431
And code generators create classes from the schema, with JAXB in Java or xsdata in Python; the code then fills objects and writes them out.

23
00:02:04.781 --> 00:02:05.643
Which one fits?

24
00:02:05.693 --> 00:02:10.385
Templates suit small and medium deliveries and teams that like to see the XML.

25
00:02:10.435 --> 00:02:13.486
X S L T suits setups where the data is already XML.

26
00:02:13.536 --> 00:02:22.423
Generated code gives type safety, but the FundsXML schema is large, so the generated code is too, and it must be regenerated for every new version.

27
00:02:23.773 --> 00:02:26.069
Every file begins with control data.

28
00:02:26.119 --> 00:02:29.913
Generate a new document ID for every file, never reuse one.

29
00:02:29.963 --> 00:02:36.755
The generation time is now; the content date is the date the data is valid for, which is usually earlier.

30
00:02:36.805 --> 00:02:38.254
Name the data supplier.

31
00:02:38.304 --> 00:02:46.204
And set the data operation: initial for a first delivery, amend for a correction, with the ID of the delivery it corrects.

32
00:02:47.554 --> 00:02:50.203
The example uses templates written in code.

33
00:02:50.253 --> 00:03:01.628
This part writes a share class: the identifiers, the name, the currency, then its prices for every date and nature, its net assets in every currency, its distributions and its flows.

34
00:03:01.678 --> 00:03:07.152
The values come from the tables you saw in the earlier films, current and released only.

35
00:03:07.202 --> 00:03:12.739
The order of the elements follows the schema exactly, because X S D sequences are strict.

36
00:03:14.089 --> 00:03:17.969
From one database, the example writes three quite different files.

37
00:03:18.019 --> 00:03:28.685
The distributor's file has an umbrella with two subfunds and a single fund, nine share classes, three days of prices with an estimated and an official value each, and no portfolio.

38
00:03:28.735 --> 00:03:35.592
The insurer's file has two funds with their month-end portfolios and the one hundred seventy-seven assets they hold.

39
00:03:35.642 --> 00:03:37.497
The data vendor gets everything.

40
00:03:38.847 --> 00:03:42.715
Several funds and dates in one file need no special code either.

41
00:03:42.765 --> 00:03:47.124
The generator writes one fund at a time, and forgets it when it is done.

42
00:03:47.174 --> 00:03:53.307
An umbrella brings its subfunds, found through the parent column, and each subfund its share classes.

43
00:03:53.357 --> 00:03:56.491
Every date and nature becomes one total asset value.

44
00:03:56.541 --> 00:04:01.940
The assets the positions refer to are collected on the way, and written once, at the end.

45
00:04:02.908 --> 00:04:05.152
The order matters more than many expect.

46
00:04:05.202 --> 00:04:09.051
Here two elements of a share class were written the wrong way round.

47
00:04:09.101 --> 00:04:15.474
The schema does not accept it: the validator reports invalid content, and names the element it expected instead.

48
00:04:15.524 --> 00:04:21.324
The fix is in the generator, which must follow the sequence of the schema exactly, element by element.

49
00:04:22.473 --> 00:04:26.305
A rule that saves many errors: leave out what you do not have.

50
00:04:26.355 --> 00:04:31.377
An empty element is not the same as a missing one, and it often fails validation.

51
00:04:31.427 --> 00:04:34.195
Never write placeholders such as N A or zero.

52
00:04:34.245 --> 00:04:43.211
In the example, the one function that builds every element returns nothing when there is no value, so an empty element cannot be written at all.

53
00:04:43.261 --> 00:04:48.283
And if a required element has no value, the problem is in the data; fix it there.

54
00:04:49.633 --> 00:04:52.075
Formats are the next source of trouble.

55
00:04:52.125 --> 00:04:58.591
Numbers use a decimal point and no thousands separators, whatever the language settings of the server.

56
00:04:58.641 --> 00:05:00.827
Dates are written year, month, day.

57
00:05:00.877 --> 00:05:02.552
Booleans are true or false.

58
00:05:02.602 --> 00:05:04.085
The file is U T F eight.

59
00:05:04.135 --> 00:05:07.790
And percentages are plain numbers, without a percent sign.

60
00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:13.521
Here is the generated file for the insurer, opened in FreeXmlToolkit.

61
00:05:13.571 --> 00:05:15.951
Validation against the schema: valid.

62
00:05:16.001 --> 00:05:18.183
The data-quality check: no errors.

63
00:05:18.233 --> 00:05:24.553
Every generated file goes through exactly these checks before it leaves the house, automatically.

64
00:05:25.343 --> 00:05:27.078
Corrections are generated too.

65
00:05:27.128 --> 00:05:34.101
A correction is a new file with its own document ID, the data operation amend, and the ID of the delivery it corrects.

66
00:05:34.151 --> 00:05:40.171
It carries the complete corrected data for those funds and that date, not just the one changed number.

67
00:05:40.221 --> 00:05:45.349
Then the receiver can simply replace what it has, as you saw in the film about loading.

68
00:05:46.699 --> 00:05:54.880
For large deliveries, write the file as a stream too: with the XML stream writer in Java, or incremental writing in Python, as here.

69
00:05:54.930 --> 00:06:04.234
The example writes one fund at a time, and reads the positions through a server-side cursor, a few hundred rows at a time, so that memory stays small.

70
00:06:04.284 --> 00:06:08.350
Very large deliveries can be split, for example one file per fund.

71
00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:10.595
And compress the file for transport.

72
00:06:11.945 --> 00:06:14.078
Partners expect different versions.

73
00:06:14.128 --> 00:06:21.627
Keep one generator and describe the differences between the versions in a table, rather than copying the whole generator.

74
00:06:21.677 --> 00:06:26.930
New elements in FundsXML are usually optional, so a newer version mostly adds fields.

75
00:06:26.980 --> 00:06:30.361
Test every version you deliver, against its own schema.

76
00:06:31.711 --> 00:06:37.939
Partners have wishes: one wants the E S G template, another only some share classes, a third no documents.

77
00:06:37.989 --> 00:06:40.486
Handle these in configuration, not in code.

78
00:06:40.536 --> 00:06:50.319
The register says which blocks and templates each partner gets, and the generator follows it; in the example, the distributor gets documents and the insurer does not.

79
00:06:50.369 --> 00:06:57.012
A copy of the generator per partner is the fastest way into the trouble you saw in the first film of this series.

80
00:06:58.362 --> 00:07:05.856
Generation usually has a short time window: between the approval of the data in the evening and the cut-off time of the partners.

81
00:07:05.906 --> 00:07:08.546
Generate funds in parallel, one fund per task.

82
00:07:08.596 --> 00:07:13.107
Start each delivery as soon as its data is approved, not at a fixed late hour.

83
00:07:13.157 --> 00:07:18.136
And measure how long it takes, so that you notice early when growth makes it too slow.

84
00:07:19.486 --> 00:07:24.349
Test with golden files: a fixed set of input data and the file it must produce.

85
00:07:24.399 --> 00:07:30.134
In the example, the six deliveries are the input, and the four expected files are the output.

86
00:07:30.184 --> 00:07:37.722
After every change, the generator runs again, and the result is compared with the golden file, then validated and checked.

87
00:07:37.772 --> 00:07:43.382
Include the difficult cases: a correction, an umbrella, a share class in a second currency.

88
00:07:44.732 --> 00:07:45.818
Automate the tests.

89
00:07:45.868 --> 00:08:01.006
In the example, every change runs through the same steps on GitHub: start the database, load the deliveries, reject the broken ones, generate the files for every partner, validate them, compare them with the golden files, and run the data-quality checks.

90
00:08:01.056 --> 00:08:05.251
A change that breaks the output is noticed before it reaches a partner.

91
00:08:06.601 --> 00:08:13.170
One more test is cheap and finds a lot: load the generated file again, with the loader of the last film.

92
00:08:13.220 --> 00:08:21.318
Every asset must be found again: by its I S I N, or, for assets without one, by the key the generator wrote as other identifier.

93
00:08:21.368 --> 00:08:26.855
In the log, the four hundred thirty assets are all known: not one new asset is created.

94
00:08:26.905 --> 00:08:29.974
And every portfolio still reconciles to the cent.

95
00:08:31.324 --> 00:08:33.827
After the checks, the file is handed over.

96
00:08:33.877 --> 00:08:39.299
It gets the agreed name, is compressed if it is large, and goes out on the agreed channel.

97
00:08:39.349 --> 00:08:42.582
Where possible, the receiver confirms that it arrived.

98
00:08:42.632 --> 00:08:50.425
And the file goes into the archive, together with its validation result, so that it can always be found again by its document ID.

99
00:08:51.775 --> 00:08:54.141
Sometimes a partner asks for a file again.

100
00:08:54.191 --> 00:08:57.823
Send the archived file, unchanged and with the same document ID.

101
00:08:57.873 --> 00:09:06.510
Do not generate it again from the database, because the data may have changed since, and the partner would receive different numbers under the same ID.

102
00:09:06.560 --> 00:09:10.307
If the content has to change, that is a correction, with a new ID.

103
00:09:11.657 --> 00:09:13.505
Finally, document the generator.

104
00:09:13.555 --> 00:09:17.362
Keep the mapping table next to the code and change both together.

105
00:09:17.412 --> 00:09:22.642
Keep a short change log for your partners: what changed in the deliveries, and from when.

106
00:09:22.692 --> 00:09:27.863
And keep one example file for every kind of delivery; the golden files are exactly that.

107
00:09:27.913 --> 00:09:32.254
When a partner asks how a field is filled, the answer is found in minutes.

108
00:09:33.604 --> 00:09:43.714
Everything you saw in this film is in the complete example: the generator, the partner register, the mapping table, and the files it writes for the three partners.

109
00:09:43.764 --> 00:09:51.318
You find it in the FundsXML examples repository on GitHub, in the folder PostgreSQL end to end, also as a single zip file.

110
00:09:51.368 --> 00:09:55.307
Change the register, run the script, and compare your own files.

111
00:09:56.657 --> 00:10:06.495
That was generating FundsXML: the target first, a clear mapping, a partner register, careful formats, and automatic tests with golden files and a round trip.

112
00:10:06.545 --> 00:10:11.597
In the last film of this series, you see how to run all this in daily operations.

113
00:10:11.647 --> 00:10:12.857
Thanks for watching.
